title One tree, one number -- the census that read two was reading a comment
prompt Continue the loop -- pull the anointed remote first, take the next lap Lindy-first crux-first, reds first, roster cold then hot
think The rota's opening pull refused on a dirty index for the second lap running: a full package sat staged from the lap before, nineteen paths. So the lap opened by closing that one, which is what the refusal is for.
think The recommend line named the crux plainly -- the dated_path census read 178, then 182 three times, then 178 twice, on what the round called an unmoved tree, and a census reporting two numbers for one tree is worth more than the ceiling set from it.
think First question worth asking: is the census non-deterministic at all? Ran it four times, once deliberately beside the live roster, and diffed the full 9,419-line reference lists. Byte-identical, 178 every time. That killed the instability hypothesis and left the tree as the only thing that could have moved.
think Then the tree. git show HEAD:context/LEXICON.md carried EXACTLY FOUR occurrences of the debrided counsel page against zero in the working tree -- the exact size of one transition -- and git diff --cached showed the scan's own comment naming that page absent from HEAD. Both transitions were in the diff the whole time.
think The second half is the one that matters past this round. dp_discovered_fixture_basenames subtracts a dated basename that appears in authored .sh/.rye/.rish/.brix and whose sprig names no file. A comment is authored code to a grep, and a debrided page's sprig names no file BY DESIGN, so writing the explanation put the page on the fixture roster and its five surviving references stopped counting -- on the same lap the ceiling rose by one to hold them. Two opposite corrections for one event, neither aware of the other.
think Measured the fix before choosing it rather than after. Stripping full-line comments drops exactly three basenames from discovery, 59 to 56. Two of them carry ZERO references anywhere in the corpus, so subtracting them subtracted nothing and re-admitting them costs nothing. The third is the real page. That is what made the strip free rather than a trade.
think Where the page belongs was already written in the file I was editing: the LISTED half holds names the tree deliberately does not carry, and three debrided names from 20260823.072824 already sit there with a paragraph saying a debrided name cannot be discovered and must be listed. The prior lap got the right subtraction by the wrong mechanism, with no record. Moving it from accident to decision is the whole repair.
think Rota row 1, Air and Saturn -- law and boundary -- read TAME_CORE, single-stranded, and Gauge. Single-stranded named the shape of the fix while I was choosing it: discovery is about one thing (what an instrument plants) and the listed roster is about one thing (what the tree deliberately lacks), and the defect was one part quietly doing the other's job.
obs I moved the tree under my own opening roster, which is exactly what the card says not to do and what REDS %221 booked. The run will report tree_moved and its verdicts describe neither tree. Not a new red -- the guard is working as designed and caught me -- yet it is mine to say plainly, and the authoritative reading for this round is the hot pass after git add, taken holding still.
obs The ceiling stays at 178 rather than falling back to 177. It would be pleasant to undo the one rise this ledger records, and 178 is what the tree measures with the subtraction made honestly, so the number stands on the reading rather than on the tidier story.
obs Proving the fix only in the passing direction would have proven nothing. Removed the strip and the control reads commented_page_free=no and commented_rye_free=no; restored it and both read yes, with trailing_comment_still_plants=yes on both sides because that leg guards against over-stripping rather than under-stripping. Removed the listing and the same tree reads 183 over its ceiling; restored, 178 under.
obs Two guards stood red on the opening roster -- geode_libraries and readme_metrics -- and both are the signature of a lap that ended at git add, since tools/hooks/pre-commit regenerates those two pages at commit. They are the wreckage the refusal names rather than defects in the tree, and the commit is what closes them.
