think The send found the ordering remote one commit ahead, and upstream_shape_scan.sh -- the tool the lap before it built for exactly this -- read verdict=diverged with a real merge base and 3 to replay, so the rebase was the ordinary kind rather than the expensive one. Its first real use answered the question it was written for.
obs The rebase conflicted on tools/fixtures/sow_leak_scan.sh, where the macOS bench had repaired the same red within hours and reached a different answer: this pier REMOVED the two corrupted entries and leaned on sow_scrub.sed one layer up, while the bench RESTORED them spelled with a bracketed character class -- the same technique the scrub itself was re-spelled with in the debride's own commit. The bracketed form is strictly stronger, since it keeps the wall catching what it was built to catch AND survives the next blanket rewrite, so I took theirs and rewrote my comment to say why rather than keeping my own weaker fix out of authorship.
obs My own fold broke three links and the roster caught it on the tree that made them. The two rows moved to a shelf one directory deeper still carrying `../tools/...` paths written for the pin, which is correct prose and a wrong fact -- verbatim is right for a row's words and wrong for its paths. Every earlier fold was safe by accident, because the four prior shelves happened to carry only `](../REDS.md)`, which resolves from both depths. Booked %247, re-anchored, readme_reach GREEN.
obs I wrote a stamp of 20260826.041500 into that row from nothing, then read the clock and found 20260826.030941. Caught it before the commit and swept every occurrence. A fabricated stamp is REDS %63's own lesson and my standing memory says read the clock, so the discipline held only because I re-read what I had written.
