think Fetching once more before the send found the ordering remote force-pushed a SECOND time in one round -- c7c7872895, verdict=rewritten, merge_base=none. upstream_shape_scan.sh, built one lap earlier for exactly this, recovered the pre-rewrite fork point from the remote-tracking reflog and printed the rebase --onto that replays 3 commits rather than 3,473. Its second real use, and it answered.
think Measured the overlap before touching anything, the way the lap before did: 53 files in my three commits, 5,092 in the rewrite, 18 intersecting. Then attempted the seated repair and got four conflicts on the first commit -- three append surfaces and one file added into a directory the molt renamed, tools/gen/season to tools/gen/chapter. All mechanical so far.
think Checked the molt's shape before inventing anything: 829 upstream basenames still carry the word season, so the rename is the DIRECTORY plus living prose rather than a basename sweep, and placing my file at tools/gen/chapter/ with its own name intact follows upstream's own surviving pattern. That was the reading that would have let the rebase finish.
obs Then the ledger conflict showed the thing that stopped the round. Upstream's spine ends at %235 and this pier's reaches %247, and %233, %234, and %235 name SIX DIFFERENT REDS -- this pier's three stamped 20260825.235254, the bench's stamped 20260826.014404, .015353, and .023323. Twelve rows, %236 through %247, exist on one side only. That is REDS %230 firing a second time, and %230 is recorded OPEN and GATE on precisely this question.
obs %230's own rule does settle it -- the earlier stamp keeps the number, so this pier holds %233-%235 and the bench's three shift to %248-%250 -- and I wrote that answer down rather than pretending the question was open. What the rule does not do is make the ACT small: applying it means renumbering three rows in a ledger this pier did not write, sweeping their citations, and carrying three commits across a vocabulary molt whose reasoning lives only inside the rewritten history. That is a decision, not a repair.
obs So I aborted the rebase rather than resolving toward a guess, and parked the package whole on refs/heads/pier/one-tree-one-number -- the shape the sync rota names for exactly this, additive and never forced. Nothing is stranded and nothing is invented. Booked %248 with the numbers that make Keaton's word cheap: three colliding numbers, twelve unseen rows, three commits, 53 files, 18 intersecting.
obs Worth saying plainly because it is the pleasant half: every guard the lap before built earned its keep within one round. upstream_shape named the shape and printed the repair; the dirty-index refusal saved the round before this one; readme_reach caught my own fold on the tree that made it. A guard that fires on its author is the only kind that was ever worth writing.
